    <paragraphs>
    <title>Course Description</title>
      <p>The goal of linear algebra is to study systems of linear equations. It is 
        difficult to describe the content informally, but there are many algebraic 
        computations that we will extend to arrays of numbers like vectors and 
        matrices. Many applications of linear algebra outside of the textbook 
        are solved using computers however we will be looking at the math behind 
        these so many of the computations will be done by hand and with the use 
        of a calculator. This means that students will:
      <ul>
        <li>Ability to use matrices to solve equations</li>
        <li>Ability to obtain linear combinations of vectors</li>
        <li>Ability to solve applications related to vector equations</li>
        <li>Ability to demonstrate understanding of linear independence</li>
        <li>Ability to find images of and geometrically describe linear transformations</li>
        <li>Ability to perform basic and advanced operations on matrices</li>
        <li>Ability to prove theorems and demonstrate concept knowledge about invertibility</li>
        <li>Ability to compute determinants using multiple methods</li>
        <li>Ability to use determinants to solve applications</li>
        <li>Ability to prove whether a set is a vector space or a subspace</li>
        <li>Ability to demonstrate understanding of spanning sets and bases</li>
        <li>Ability to use dimension and rank to demonstrate understanding of vector spaces</li>
        <li>Ability to find eigenvalues and eigenvectors in order to diagonalize matrices</li>
        <li>Ability to find inner products to demonstrate understanding of orthogonality, normality, and factorizations</li>
      </ul></p>
      <p>To meet these course outcomes students will be specifically assessed on the following standards:</p>
      </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Student Learning Outcomes</title>
      <p>Habit Standards assessed throughout the entire semester
      <ul>
        <li>LA-1 GP (written practice)</li>
        <li>LA-2 Communication</li>
        <li>LA-3 Reflections</li>
        <li>LA-4 Participation</li>
      </ul></p>
    <p>Content Outcomes assessed within specific content modules (complete descriptions can be found in Canvas)
    <ul>
      <li>LSAI-1 Linear Equations</li>
      <li>LSAI-2 Linear Independence</li>
      <li>LSAI-3 Linear Transformations</li>
      <li>LSAI-4 Applications of Linear Systems</li>
      <li>MD-1 Matrix Operations</li>
      <li>MD-2 Matrix Inverses</li>
      <li>MD-3 Determinants and Cramer's Rule</li>
      <li>VS-1 Vector Spaces and Subspaces</li>
      <li>VS-2 Linearly Independent Set and Coordinate Systems</li>
      <li>VS-3 Bases, Dimension and Rank</li>
      <li>VS-4 Change of Basis</li>
      <li>EIPO-1 Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</li>
      <li>EIPO-2 Diagonalization</li>
      <li>EIPO-3 Inner Products</li>
      <li>EIPO-4 Orthogonality</li>
      <li>EIPO-5 Gram-Schmidt Process</li>
      <li>EIPO-6 Least Squares</li>
    </ul>
    </p></paragraphs>
  
